[1mNAME[0m
       senpai - your everyday IRC student

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1msenpai [22m[options...]

[1mOPTIONS[0m
       [1m-config [22m<path>
           Use a different path for the configuration file.

       [1m-nickname [22m<nickname>
           Advanced.  Nick name to connect as. Overrides the
           configuration item of the same name.

       [1m-debug[0m
           Advanced. Show all IRC messages that are received
           from/sent to the server.

       [1m-version[0m
           Display version info.

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       senpai is an IRC client made for bouncers.   It  sup‐
       ports the newest IRC extensions, such as:

       •   [4mCHATHISTORY[24m,  senpai  fetches  history  from  the
           server instead of keeping logs,
       •   [4m@+typing[24m, senpai shows when others are  typing  a
           message,
       •   [4mBOUNCER[24m,  senpai connects to all your networks at
           once automatically,
       •   [4mSEARCH[24m,  senpai  can  search   messages   quickly
           server-side,
       •   [4mFILEHOST[24m, senpai can upload files to the bouncer,
       •   and more to come!

[1mCONFIGURATION[0m
       senpai searches for it in the following location:

           $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/senpai/senpai.scfg

       If  unset,  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME defaults to [1m~/.config [22mor
       the platform's equivalent

       For information about the configuration  format,  see
       [1msenpai[22m(5).

       If the configuration file does not exist, a setup as‐
       sistant will create one for you.

[1mUSER INTERFACE[0m
       The  user  interface  of  senpai  consists of several
       parts.

       On the left, the [1mbuffer list[22m, shows joined  channels.
       The  special  buffer [1mhome [22mis where server notices are
       shown. The list can be  put  on  the  bottom  of  the
       screen  with  a  configuration option. Buffers can be
       closed with the mouse middle click, or the [4mpart[24m  com‐
       mand.

       On  the  right, the [1mmember list[22m, shows members joined
       to the current channel.

       On the bottom, the [1minput field [22mis where you  type  in
       messages  or  commands  (see  [1mCOMMANDS[22m).  By default,
       when you type a message, senpai will inform others in
       the channel that you are typing.

       On the row above, the [1mstatus line [22m(or... just a  line
       if  nothing  is happening...) is where typing indica‐
       tors are shown (e.g. "dan- is typing...").

       Finally, the message [1mtimeline  [22mis  displayed  on  the
       rest of the screen.

       senpai supports the mouse almost everywhere (clicking
       on  nicks,  buffers, members, links; resizing panels;
       and features "buttons"). On supported terminals,  the
       cursor  will  turn  into a hand when over a clickable
       element.

[1mSELECTING TEXT[0m
       In order to select text  with  a  mouse,  hold  SHIFT
       while  clicking  and dragging the mouse. [1mClicking and[0m
       [1mdragging without holding SHIFT will not work, as sen‐[0m
       [1mpai eats these events for eg selecting channels.[0m

[1mOPENING LINKS[0m
       In order to open links, refer to your  terminal  man‐
       ual.  On  most  terminals,  opening  links is done by
       holding CTRL, or SHIFT, while clicking the  link.  On
       the  [1mfoot  [22mterminal,  links can be opened by pressing
       CTRL+SHIFT+O.

       A simple left click with no modifiers will make  sen‐
       pai  try to preview the link, or open it. In order to
       skip the preview, open the link with a  modifier,  as
       specified above, instead of an unmodified left click.

